<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Satsuma Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Kagoshima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The powerful Shimazu domain of southern Kyushu, overlord of Ryukyu and, with Choshu, the driving force behind the Meiji Restoration.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu</a> had fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, but by negotiation they kept Satsuma, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osumi-province">Osumi</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyuga-province">Hyuga</a>, assessed at about 770,000 koku and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>. In 1609 the domain invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom</a> and made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Satsuma had an unusually large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> class, many living as rural warriors, and a strict youth training system. Its control of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu</a> gave it a hidden window on Chinese trade, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zusho-hiromichi">Zusho Hiromichi</a> restored its finances in the 1830s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-nariakira">Shimazu Nariakira</a> built Western-style furnaces, shipyards and factories at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuseikan">Shuseikan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Namamugi incident a British squadron bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> in 1863. Satsuma then modernised, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> in 1866, and its men <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Okubo Toshimichi</a> led the Restoration of 1868. It became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a> in 1871; Saigo's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> of 1877 was crushed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/satsuma-domain.jpg" alt="Satsuma Domain" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Mass Ave 975 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kagoshimajo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kagoshima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shimazu Iehisa, Shimazu Nariakira, Shimazu Tadayoshi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kagoshima Domain, Satsuma han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=satsuma-domain" aria-label="Satsuma Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8047 1027 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Satsuma Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8367 1283L8367 1283M8367 1283L8530 1145M8367 1283L8529 1145M8367 1283L8372 1471"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8367 1283h0M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0M8372 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8367 1283h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8367 1283h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=satsuma-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
